  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 228 PM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated to scattered showers continue this \nafternoon into this evening as an initial cold front begins its \npassage through the region. Drier air directly behind the front\nwill filter into northern New York and Vermont swiftly this \nevening, becoming enhanced by the of the right exit region of an\nincoming elongated jet streak, yielding dry conditions later \nthis evening through the overnight period. However, with clouds \nslow to scour out due to lower-level moisture becoming trapped \nbelow a low- to mid-level inversion, temperatures will remain on\nthe mild side. Expect lows in the low 40s to low 50s. \n\nDry conditions remain steady through tomorrow morning and even\nearly tomorrow afternoon as a secondary cold front and parent\nlow are slow to make eastward progress off the Eastern Great\nLakes. However, by mid-afternoon, isolated to scattered showers\nreturn, particularly for Lake-adjacent areas, the Adirondacks\nwhere upslope flow will overcome an overall lack of forcing, and\nalong the VT/NH border as a low develops along today's initial\ncold front that looks to stall along the New England Coastline.\nRainfall rates are anticipated to be light, with QPF generally\nremaining under 0.1\" through tomorrow night. Tomorrow's highs\nwill be a bit cooler than today, though still classify as\nabove-normal with values in the low 50s to mid 60s. Lows\ntomorrow night will then largely be in the 40s with upper 30s\nacross higher elevations. \n\nThe passage of the secondary cold front tomorrow night will see\ntemperatures fall much closer to normal than we have been over\nthe last several days. Highs Saturday will only reach the 40s\nand 50s with additional chances for isolated to scattered\nshowers across higher elevations as moist, cyclonic flow remains\nin place beneath an upper-level low moving into the Northeast.\nLows Saturday night will then fall largely to the 30s and 40s\nwith pockets of upper 20s across the Adirondacks and Greens.\nSunday will remain seasonably chilly with additional potential\nfor isolated, high-elevation showers, which could contain some\nsnowflakes, and highs in the 30s to low 40s across higher \nelevations and upper 40s to low 50s in valley areas. Lows Sunday\nnight will then fall largely to the 20s and 30s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: \n\nAdditional chances for precipitation come mostly for Monday and\nWednesday next week as we remain in a more unsettled pattern. \nHowever, given the recent dry conditions, all precipitation will\nbe beneficial and not pose any significant impacts.\nTemperatures will remain more consistently near normal next week\nwith highs largely in the 40s and 50s and lows in the 20s and\n30s.",
